# 📩 Email & SMS Spam Detector

[![CI](https://github.com/YOUR_GITHUB_USERNAME/spam-detector/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/YOUR_GITHUB_USERNAME/spam-detector/actions/workflows/ci.yml)
![Python](https://img.shields.io/badge/python-3.9%2B-blue)
![License](https://img.shields.io/badge/license-MIT-green)

A machine-learning project that classifies SMS and email text as **spam** or **ham** (genuine).
It compares three classic algorithms on TF-IDF features, tunes them with cross-validation, and ships with a
command-line tool, a web interface, tests and continuous integration.

![Web interface](docs/screenshots/check_spam.png)

```text
Input : Congratulations! You won Rs 10000. Claim your prize now!
Output: SPAM      (spam probability 98.7%)

Input : Hey, are you coming to college tomorrow?
Output: NOT SPAM  (spam probability 0.1%)
```

## Results

Tuned with 5-fold stratified cross-validation on the training set (4,135 messages); scores below are on a
**held-out test set of 1,034 messages** that was used only once. Spam is the positive class.

| Model | CV F1 | Accuracy | Precision | Recall | F1 | ROC AUC |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Naive Bayes** (selected) | 0.9526 | 0.9865 | 0.9606 | 0.9313 | 0.9457 | 0.9925 |
| Logistic Regression | 0.9400 | 0.9807 | 0.9302 | 0.9160 | 0.9231 | 0.9948 |
| SVM (calibrated linear) | 0.9468 | 0.9826 | 0.9520 | 0.9084 | 0.9297 | 0.9962 |

The model is chosen by cross-validated F1 (not by test score), so the test set stays an unbiased final check.
The differences between models are small (cross-validation standard deviation is about 0.005 to 0.012), so
all three are good; SVM and Logistic Regression rank messages slightly better (higher ROC AUC), while Naive
Bayes makes the best hard decisions at the default 0.5 threshold.

![Model comparison](reports/figures/model_comparison.png)

More figures (ROC and precision-recall curves, top spam words) are in [`reports/figures`](reports/figures), and
the full numbers are in [`reports/metrics.json`](reports/metrics.json).

### Honest check: messages the models have never seen

The original test set comes from the same old dataset as the training data, so it is optimistic. To test
generalisation, 60 **hand-written** messages (30 spam, 30 genuine) that are *not* in the dataset were
scored once, and never used for tuning: modern Indian-style scams (fake KYC, UPI refund, courier fee,
job and investment offers), Hinglish chat, and genuine automatic messages such as OTPs and bank alerts.

| Model | Accuracy | Precision | Recall | F1 | Spam caught | Genuine flagged |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Naive Bayes (selected) | 0.767 | 0.900 | 0.600 | 0.720 | 18 / 30 | 2 / 30 |
| Logistic Regression | 0.867 | 0.958 | 0.767 | 0.852 | 23 / 30 | 1 / 30 |
| SVM | 0.817 | 0.952 | 0.667 | 0.784 | 20 / 30 | 1 / 30 |

**Takeaways:** scores fall sharply on modern messages, so the 98.7% test accuracy does not carry over to
today's scams (the training data is old UK/Singapore SMS). Fake-job and investment offers are missed most
often, and genuine OTP or bank-debit alerts can be wrongly flagged. The model chosen by cross-validation
(Naive Bayes) is *not* the best on this harder set; Logistic Regression is. The set has only 60 messages, so
treat these numbers as a rough guide. The fix is more recent and Indian training data, not tuning on this
set (that would make it no longer "unseen"). Per-message results: `reports/unseen_results.csv`.

## Features
- Text cleaning built into the model pipeline (links, money and numbers become tokens), so there is no
  difference between training and prediction.
- Three models: Multinomial Naive Bayes, Logistic Regression, linear SVM (probability-calibrated).
- Hyper-parameter search (`GridSearchCV`) over TF-IDF settings and each model's main parameter.
- Metrics: accuracy, precision, recall, F1, ROC-AUC, PR-AUC, confusion matrix; error analysis in
  `reports/misclassified_examples.csv`.
- Web app with spam probability, confidence level, answers of all three models side by side, word-level
  explanation, adjustable threshold, clickable examples and a performance dashboard.
- Command-line tool, Streamlit web app, 29 unit tests, ruff linting, GitHub Actions on Python 3.10 to 3.12.

## Quick start

```bash
git clone https://github.com/YOUR_GITHUB_USERNAME/spam-detector.git
cd spam-detector

python3 -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate            # Windows: venv\Scripts\activate
pip install -e ".[app,dev]"

spam-detector train                 # under a minute; creates models/ and reports/
```

### Classify messages from the terminal
```bash
spam-detector predict "Free entry! Text WIN to 80086 now" "See you at the lecture tomorrow"
spam-detector interactive           # type messages one by one
```

### Web interface (localhost)
```bash
streamlit run app/streamlit_app.py  # opens http://localhost:8501
```

<p align="center">
  <img src="docs/screenshots/performance.png" width="48%" alt="Performance tab">
  <img src="docs/screenshots/unseen_test.png" width="48%" alt="Unseen-message test tab">
</p>

### Use it in your own Python code
```python
from spamdetector import SpamClassifier

classifier = SpamClassifier.load()
result = classifier.predict("URGENT! Your account is blocked. Click http://bit.ly/abc")
print(result.label, result.spam_probability, result.confidence_label)
print(classifier.explain("URGENT! Your account is blocked. Click http://bit.ly/abc"))
```

## Project structure
```
spam-detector/
├── src/spamdetector/
│   ├── config.py          paths and settings
│   ├── data.py            load, validate, split
│   ├── preprocessing.py   text cleaning
│   ├── models.py          the 3 pipelines and their tuning grids
│   ├── train.py           tune, evaluate, save
│   ├── evaluate.py        metrics and plots
│   ├── predict.py         SpamClassifier for new messages (probability, confidence, explanation)
│   ├── unseen.py          evaluation on the hand-written unseen messages
│   └── cli.py             `spam-detector` command
├── app/streamlit_app.py   web interface
├── tests/                 pytest unit tests
├── data/raw/spam.csv      SMS Spam Collection (see data/README.md)
├── data/unseen/           60 hand-written messages used only for the unseen-message test
├── reports/               metrics, comparison table, figures
├── docs/                  model card, project report, viva questions
├── scripts/               download_data.py, personalize.py
└── .github/               CI workflow, issue and PR templates
```

## Development
```bash
make test     # pytest
make lint     # ruff
make train    # retrain
make app      # web interface
```

## Limitations
- English SMS from the 2000s. On the unseen-message test, modern scams are often missed and genuine OTP or
  bank alerts can be flagged (see above).
- Only message text is used (no sender, link reputation or attachments).
- Single dataset and a single train/test split; see [docs/MODEL_CARD.md](docs/MODEL_CARD.md).

